# Let the assistant reset the mic array over USB without running as root.
#
#   sudo cp 99-respeaker.rules /etc/udev/rules.d/
#   sudo udevadm control --reload-rules && sudo udevadm trigger
#
# Scoped to exactly one device by vendor:product — this is not a blanket USB
# permission, it is "anyone on this machine may reset this one microphone".
#
# Why MODE 0666 rather than a group:
#
#   GROUP="audio" needs the service user to be in that group, and Ubuntu no
#   longer puts desktop users there now that PipeWire handles audio. Adding
#   them works, but group membership only applies to a NEW login session,
#   and a lingering systemd user manager does not get one on boot.
#
#   TAG+="uaccess" is the modern answer, but it grants access to the user of
#   an ACTIVE SEAT. This appliance boots with nobody logged in — which is the
#   precise case the reset exists for — so there is no seat to grant from.
#
#   0666 on one microphone, on a machine with one user, is the right trade.
#   Anyone who can reach this box to abuse it can already unplug the thing.
SUBSYSTEM=="usb", ATTR{idVendor}=="2886", ATTR{idProduct}=="001a", MODE="0666"

# The USB hub itself.
#
# A protocol reset is not enough for this microphone — measured on this
# build, USBDEVFS_RESET, authorized-toggling and driver unbind/rebind all
# leave it returning EIO after a warm reboot. None of them cut VBUS, and its
# XMOS processor only recovers when power actually goes away.
#
# The software equivalent of unplugging it is per-port power switching, and
# uhubctl issues that as a control transfer to the HUB, not to the
# microphone. So the rule above grants nothing useful for it; the hub needs
# its own. 0bda:5411 is the Realtek controller inside the Sabrent hub — three
# of them appear, one per internal tier, and all three match this one rule.
#
# Check yours before trusting this line:  lsusb | grep -i hub
SUBSYSTEM=="usb", ATTR{idVendor}=="0bda", ATTR{idProduct}=="5411", MODE="0666"
